this week I took part in the climate  strike in Bristol joining around 30,000  others in the rain on college green I  traveled back from London to take part  in the strike because Bristol is my  hometown and because this is a seriously  important moment for the climate  movement in the UK and the reason for  that is because of this girl greater Tim  Berg  she has brought hundreds of thousands of  people together to protest the lack of  action from governments on climate  change starting from a one-person  protest she found herself this week  leading a march of tens of thousands  through the city of Bristol as someone  with relevant scientific training I  totally understand the criticisms of  grete Tim Berg and her role in the  climate movement I've hesitated to make  public comment on her in the past  precisely because of these complaints  wouldn't it be better to use a scientist  as a figurehead isn't the movement  rather cult-like does greater actually  understand the science or if she's just  parroting lines from a school textbook  but as I stood maybe 50 metres from this  girl who was greeted with deafening  roars to the stage I was struck almost  immediately by a few things when  speaking she had an undeniable presence  it wasn't showy it wasn't even really  charismatic but it was entrancing I  immediately understood why so many  people have followed her in the climate  strike movement her speech was delivered  calmly quietly and simply it was a  climate message stripped down to its  bare essentials the problem with talking  about climate change is that it is a  complicated topic when scientists speak  to the public or to government on the  issue we tend to get more focused on the  trees and less on the forests more about  the details and less about the big  picture  we give uncertainties and caveats and  alternative explanations and those  things are not compelling to government  ministers or tea up average voter  they obfuscate the message you're trying  to get across these people don't have  scientific training they need an  abstract not a full review paper and the  thing is for quite some time now the  science has been settled there's  obviously window dressing to be done and  lots of uncertainties still to be nailed  down the review  paper is far from finished but the  abstract conclusion is human activity  has warmed the planet with unprecedented  speed and will do so at an increasing  rate if we don't change our activities  soon and that warming will affect  everyone not just scientists it will  affect everyday people from all walks of  life and from every country around the  world we are all stakeholders in our  collective response to human-made  climate change so with that in mind does  it matter that the figurehead of this  movement is a teenage girl who hasn't  read all the scientific papers no she's  a teenage girl who was repeating the  abstract conclusions of tens of  thousands of scientists and outright  telling governments to listen to those  scientists she's not an authority and  recognizes that but she will be affected  by climate change in her lifetime and so  has just as much a right to be heard on  this issue as the scientists but she is  not and she herself is at pains to  stress that she is not the beginning and  end of this movement it extends beyond  her to the scientific community to the  voting population she is just a spark  the people at the March with a flame and  the flame is what's important a spark  will not force governments to take  meaningful action whether that spark is  a climate scientist or a teenager a  flame however cannot be ignored and  Greta's spark lit tinder around the  world the simplicity of her message the  calm she exudes roused people to action  in a way that climate scientists have  not those people at the March were  inspired by her and she was inspired by  the scientists she listened to this  movement this March was based on science  it just took the simplicity of greta's  message to get people to listen the  March itself was uniquely Bristolian I  was really proud to come from the West  country seeing all the unique and funny  and creative ways that people were  contributing to this movement not least  the fact that even horrible weather  didn't put a dampener on the event  although we did slightly turned collard  green into college mud sorry Bristol  City Council the councillor for striking  for climate  trying to retake the fortress of Verdun  Bristol has long been at the forefront  of demanding action on climate change in  the UK and this March will have only  strengthened the momentum of this  movement in the city further just last  week Bristol Airport was denied  permission to build a new runway after  extensive lobbying by this movement and  a couple of days ago London Heathrow was  denied the same expansion after judges  ruled that the UK must uphold its  commitments to the Paris agreement and  with tens of thousands of people taking  to the streets and many many more  cheering from their offices and from  scaffolding it really felt like change  is coming and the Bristol was going to  be at the forefront in studying and  covering climate change it's easy to  feel a sense of hopelessness and it's  important to realize that the situation  we find ourselves in is pretty dire we  face a difficult century full of tough  choices but it's not hopeless one of my  favorite quotes comes from Desmond Tutu  the Archbishop of Capetown during the  time when the system of apartheid in  South Africa was first broken down I  would hope I mean that the world would  realize that there is no situation that  is not transferrable  that there is no situation of which we  can say this is absolutely totally  devoid of hope  of course that is work people thought  about South Africa this March showed me  that there is a clear demand for action  and that the demand is growing louder  and louder and producing results these  results show that there is hope we don't  have to resign ourselves to the worst  case scenario and all of this is only  possible because of the following of a  single girl whose demand is just that  the public listens to the science  arguably this teenager from Sweden has  produced a more meaningful change in the  public's demands from their governments  than decades of scientists lobbying  those same governments would I rather  that the public and the government's  paid Heat those scientists warnings  directly and could access and understand  the scientific papers on the subject  absolutely yes but if greater timbo can  produce this momentum this spark by  simply and repeatedly making the same  points then that works by me let's hope  that this particular flame burns on and  burns brighter because we all need it to  thanks for watching I'll see you in the  next one
 

